Restart stage with the three jump buttons, from the keyboard.

The stage module already restarts the stage when A+B+C arrive in the same frame
(src/game.c, the give-up branch, matching GAME.C:360), and the reference frontend
feeds exactly that synthetic pad frame when its own restart flag is set
(`swdata1 = 0x70`, WINEXE/WINMAIN.C:2418).  This patch feeds it from the live pad
as soon as all three jump buttons are held, once per press, so the combination is
usable with a keyboard - which cannot land three key edges in one 16.7 ms frame.

NOT APPLIED.  This port has no gamepad input yet, and adding that is the proper
fix: a pad delivers all three button-downs in one poll, so the module's own check
works unchanged.  Keep this patch for the case that the keyboard route is wanted
anyway.

Apply from the project root:

    patch -p1 < patches/restart_stage_keyboard.patch

Undo it again with:

    patch -p1 -R < patches/restart_stage_keyboard.patch

See docs/restart_stage.md for the whole story.

--- a/src/frontend/main.c
+++ b/src/frontend/main.c
@@ -275,6 +275,26 @@
         padStatus.Release = lastPadOn & ~padStatus.On;
         /* A running stage demo feeds its recorded pad instead of the live one. */
         Attract_OverridePad(&joy1, &joy2);
+        /* Restart stage.  The stage module already does it: its give-up branch
+           (GAME.C:360, src/game.c) reloads the stage when all three jump buttons
+           arrive in the same frame - which is also what the reference frontend
+           feeds when its own restart flag is set (`swdata1 = 0x70`,
+           WINEXE/WINMAIN.C:2418).  On a keyboard the three edges almost never
+           land in one 16.7 ms frame, so the synthetic A+B+C frame is fed here as
+           soon as the three are held, and only once per press. */
+        if (!Attract_IsDemo()) {
+            static int restartHeld;
+
+            if ((joy1.b.h & 0x70) == 0x70) {
+                if (!restartHeld) {
+                    restartHeld = 1;
+                    joy1.b.h = 0x70;
+                    joy1.b.l = 0x70;
+                }
+            } else {
+                restartHeld = 0;
+            }
+        }
         /* A running movie is advanced here rather than from the state that
            started it, so the player keeps going no matter which DLL is on the
            screen polling Movie_Stat(). */
